  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Fixes a hole in prediction that only shows up once a real client joins a real authority: there was no\nway for a game to say which of its bodies are predicted, so a correctly written client could join a\nsession, draw the world, and never send a single command.\n\nAn ASSIGN gave its entity `LocallyControlled` and stopped there. `NetPredictionSystem` matches\n`LocallyControlled, Predicted, Position3D, Velocity3D` and `NetInputSendSystem3D` matches\n`LocallyControlled, NetworkInput3D` — and nothing in this library ever added `Predicted` or\n`NetworkInput3D`. Both systems ran every tick and matched nothing, which raises no error, so the\nsymptom was a player whose camera pitched up and down while movement, look, fire and interact all did\nnothing, and an authority that saw somebody stand still for a whole session.\n\n### Added\n\n- `NetSpawnRegistry.Add` takes an optional `assign` callback, run on the entity the authority hands\n  this client. It is where a game turns its avatar into a predicted body, next to the factory that\n  built the archetype. Registering without one is still valid — an avatar drawn from snapshots is\n  right for some games — so the library reports the case rather than refusing it.\n- `NetSpawnRegistry.TryGetAssign`, and `NetSpawnSystem.LastAssignedCount`.\n- `NetPredictionSystem.UnpredictableAvatarCount`: avatars this client controls but cannot predict.\n  Zero in a working client and zero before the first assignment; anything higher is a client that will\n  send no commands for as long as it runs. Assert on it in a test or put it on a debug overlay. It is\n  computed only on ticks that predicted nothing.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- `NetSpawnSystem` now runs the assigned kind's `assign` callback after `AssignLocal`, so the avatar\n  carries what prediction reads before the snapshot that follows it needs somewhere to record a\n  correction.",
